What authorized sources of information did you use to complete the
assignment? COPY THE LIST of authorized sources of information given
in the "Policies" web page (also available on armlab in the file
~cos217/AuthorizedSourcesList ) to this section of the readme file,
and edit it as appropriate.

You may consult the course's lectures, precepts, website, textbooks and manuals (including the man pages from the armlab cluster). These sources should be acknowledged in your readme file, but do not have to be cited specifically (e.g., with a page number or URL) unless copied verbatim.

Consulted lecture and precept notes

You may consult with the course's instructors in class, in office hours, in separate meetings, and on Ed. You may consult with your own preceptor via email. These sources should be acknowledged by name in your readme file.

Consulted Ed discussion posts

You may consult with the McGraw tutors for conceptual help about course topics (but not assignment-specific details) and with the COS Intro LabTAs for debugging help (but not general conceptual issues). These sources should be acknowledged by name in your readme file.

You may consult with peers taking the course (or those who took the course in a prior semester) only about conceptual topics. Specifically, this means you may not discuss assignment-specific details in any form: code, code comments, pseudo-code, flow charts, outlines, diagrams, natural language prose descriptions of any of the above, etc. This applies to any medium: verbally, in writing or email or texting or messaging, and on social media including a course groupchat or Discord or our course's Ed Discussion.

You may not search the web for solutions (whether full or partial solutions to the programming assignments or to related problems) or hints for solving the course assignments. It is permissible to search the web for conceptual assistance on course topics not in any way specific to the assignment, but in the past we have found students are often led astray by such queries — developing bad habits or even factual misconceptions — so we'd strongly prefer that you use the extensive set of material and personal resources from within the course. We will not respond favorably to regrade requests or general complaints when the course's customs, styles, and patterns are not followed due to having learned otherwise from external sources.

Searched up makefile error and typedef functionality

You may consult with a Large Language Model or other Generative AI for assistance with conceptual topics for the class and for help with the assignments, subject to the full Generative AI policy below. Note that we would not be surprised if you were similarly led astray by LLM responses as students have been with Google searches, but this remains an open question.

You may not provide any help that would be a violation to receive based on these policies.


